Subject: WTF on Phoenix: ThreadCondition::timedWait waits on the monotonic clock (timed waits never timed out)

ThreadCondition is PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER-initialised and timedWait() passes a WallTime
deadline to pthread_cond_timedwait(), which reads it on the condition variable's clock. POSIX's
default clock is CLOCK_REALTIME; libphoenix's is CLOCK_MONOTONIC (kept on purpose: the wall clock
jumps from 1970 to the present during boot). A wall-clock deadline read on the monotonic clock
lies some 56 years ahead, so every timed wait in WebKit -- ParkingLot's timed parks and through
them Condition::waitFor/waitUntil, BinarySemaphore::waitFor, the IPC layer's timeouts -- lasted
until the next signal. First seen as <video> never reaching its end (B8: the FFmpeg engine's
packet queues wait 20 ms for a packet and would then drain the decoder; after the last packet
nothing signals them).

On Phoenix the remaining interval is waited with pthread_cond_clockwait(CLOCK_MONOTONIC), the
condition variable's own clock, so a step of the wall clock (boot, NTP) does not stretch or cut
the wait; ParkingLot re-checks its own deadline after every wake-up. Other platforms keep
pthread_cond_timedwait().

diff --git a/Source/WTF/wtf/posix/ThreadingPOSIX.cpp b/Source/WTF/wtf/posix/ThreadingPOSIX.cpp
--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/posix/ThreadingPOSIX.cpp
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/posix/ThreadingPOSIX.cpp
@@ -698,6 +698,29 @@
         return true;
     }
 
+#if OS(PHOENIX)
+    // libphoenix's condition variables wait on CLOCK_MONOTONIC (POSIX's default is
+    // CLOCK_REALTIME) and pthread_cond_timedwait() reads the deadline on that clock: a wall-clock
+    // deadline would lie decades ahead and every timed wait (ParkingLot, Condition::waitFor)
+    // would last until a signal. Wait for the same interval on the monotonic clock, which also
+    // ignores steps of the wall clock (boot, NTP); ParkingLot re-checks its own deadline.
+    double remaining = std::max(0.0, (absoluteTime - WallTime::now()).value());
+    if (remaining > 1E9) {
+        // decades: as good as no deadline (and no time_t overflow below)
+        wait(mutex);
+        return true;
+    }
+    timespec targetTime;
+    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &targetTime);
+    time_t remainingSeconds = static_cast<time_t>(remaining);
+    targetTime.tv_sec += remainingSeconds;
+    targetTime.tv_nsec += static_cast<long>((remaining - remainingSeconds) * 1E9);
+    if (targetTime.tv_nsec >= 1000000000) {
+        targetTime.tv_sec++;
+        targetTime.tv_nsec -= 1000000000;
+    }
+    return !pthread_cond_clockwait(&m_condition, &mutex.impl(), CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &targetTime);
+#else
     double rawSeconds = absoluteTime.secondsSinceEpoch().value();
 
     time_t timeSeconds = static_cast<time_t>(rawSeconds);
@@ -708,6 +731,7 @@
     targetTime.tv_nsec = timeNanoseconds;
 
     return pthread_cond_timedwait(&m_condition, &mutex.impl(), &targetTime) == 0;
+#endif
 }
 
 void ThreadCondition::signal()
